<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection
<br class="ltx_break"><span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text">Zhixiong Chen and Arumugam Nallanathan are with the School of Electronic Engineering and Computer Science, Queen Mary University of London, London, U.K. (emails: {zhixiong.chen, a.nallanathan}@qmul.ac.uk).</span></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text">Jiangzhou Wang is with the National Mobile Communications Research Laboratory, Southeast University, Nanjing, China (email: j.z.wang@seu.edu.cn).</span></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text">Hyundong Shin is with the Department of Electronics and Information Convergence Engineering, Kyung Hee University, Yongin-si, Gyeonggido 17104, Republic of Korea (e-mail: hshin@khu.ac.kr).</span></span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhixiong Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiangzhou Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text">Hyundong Shin, ,
and Arumugam Nallanathan, 


</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The deployment of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) for reliable and energy-efficient data collection from spatially distributed devices holds great promise in supporting diverse Internet of Things (IoT) applications. Nevertheless, the limited endurance and communication range of UAVs necessitate intelligent trajectory planning. While reinforcement learning (RL) has been extensively explored for UAV trajectory optimization, its interactive nature entails high costs and risks in real-world environments. Offline RL mitigates these issues but remains susceptible to unstable training and heavily rely on expert-quality datasets. To address these challenges, we formulate a joint UAV trajectory planning and resource allocation problem to maximize energy efficiency of data collection. The resource allocation subproblem is first transformed into an equivalent linear programming formulation and solved optimally with polynomial-time complexity. Then, we propose a large language model (LLM)-empowered critic-regularized decision transformer (DT) framework, termed LLM-CRDT, to learn effective UAV control policies. In LLM-CRDT, we incorporate critic networks to regularize the DT model training, thereby integrating the sequence modeling capabilities of DT with critic-based value guidance to enable learning effective policies from suboptimal datasets. Furthermore, to mitigate the data-hungry nature of transformer models, we employ a pre-trained LLM as the transformer backbone of the DT model and adopt a parameter-efficient fine-tuning strategy, i.e., LoRA, enabling rapid adaptation to UAV control tasks with small-scale dataset and low computational overhead. Extensive simulations demonstrate that LLM-CRDT outperforms benchmark online and offline RL methods, achieving up to 36.7% higher energy efficiency than the current state-of-the-art DT approaches.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6><span id="id2" class="ltx_text">
Data collection, decision transformer, large language models, unmanned aerial vehicle, offline reinforcement learning.
</span>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid development of the Internet of Things (IoT) has driven the emergence of innovative applications such as smart cities and environmental monitoring, reshaping diverse aspects of daily life and industrial operations.These applications rely on large-scale deployments of spatially distributed IoT devices, such as sensors, to continuously monitor areas of interest and collect valuable data for analysis and decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Reliable data collection from these dispersed sensing nodes is therefore critical to unlocking the full potential of IoT systems, particularly in regions where fixed network infrastructure is impractical or prohibitively costly. In this context, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) offer a compelling solution by leveraging their deployment flexibility and precise manoeuvrability to complement ground networks and deliver ubiquitous connectivity for IoT devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Serving as aerial mobile base stations, UAVs can efficiently harvest data from distributed devices and relay it to cloud or edge servers for further processing, making them well-suited for mission-critical applications such as disaster response and precision farming.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the aforementioned advantages, the limited flight endurance and communication range of UAVs pose critical challenges for IoT data collection. Towards this end, extensive research has focused on optimizing UAV trajectory planning and wireless resource management to balance coverage, latency, and energy efficiency throughout the mission. Specifically, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, an energy-constrained UAV was dispatched to collect data from a sparse IoT sensor network, where approximation and heuristic algorithms were designed to optimize the UAV’s hovering positions and durations for maximizing the collected data volume. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, an iterative algorithm and a dynamic programming-based approach were respectively proposed to minimize data collection completion time through jointly optimizing UAV’s trajectory and wireless resource allocation. By employing convex optimization techniques for UAV trajectory design, the work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> minimized the data collection time in a multi-UAV setting. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, an ant colony algorithm was developed to minimize the age of information of data collected from ground sensor nodes. Considering the security threats in UAV-aided wireless networks, a short-packet transmission scheme was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to ensure both the freshness and security of collected data, wherein a successive convex optimization-based approach was employed to optimize the UAV’s trajectory. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, convex stochastic programming and fractional programming approaches were respectively investigated to optimize the UAV’s trajectory and maximize the energy efficiency of data collection tasks. Although these traditional optimization and heuristic-based offline methods effectively improved data collection performance, they typically require perfect channel state information over the entire time horizon. This hinders their practical deployment, as IoT networks usually operate in highly dynamic wireless environments where communication conditions change unpredictably.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limitations of traditional methods, reinforcement learning (RL) has been widely employed to learn UAV trajectory planning and resource allocation policies through interactions with the environment, eliminating the need for prior knowledge of channel state information and other environmental dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Specifically, deep Q-network-based approaches were developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to learn UAV flight path planning policies, where the flight path was discretized and the UAV only required to select actions from a finite action space. To enable more fine-grained control, the deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG) algorithm was adopted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to directly optimize the UAV control policies within a continuous action space, thereby enhancing manoeuvrability. Moreover, the twin delayed deep deterministic policy gradient (TD3) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> builds upon DDPG by incorporating twin critics, delayed policy updates, and target policy smoothing, leading to more accurate value estimation and reduced variance. Accordingly, TD3-based UAV control policies was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, achieving a more stable learning process and higher data collection rewards than DDPG. To address the low exploration efficiency caused by the deterministic policies in DDPG and TD3, soft actor-critic (SAC)-based UAV control approaches was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, employing a stochastic policy to facilitate better exploration in wireless environments with sparse rewards and complex dynamics. Despite their flexibility and effectiveness, the above approaches are all online RL methods that operate in a trial-and-error manner and require extensive real-time interactions with the environment. This makes them less practical for UAV-enabled IoT data collection due to the high operational costs and risks associated with continuous exploration. Moreover, the sample inefficiency of online RL demands a large number of interactions before converging to a high-performing policy, further limiting their applicability in large-scale IoT scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle the limitations of online RL, offline RL offers a promising alternative by learning effective policies entirely from pre-collected datasets. This paradigm enables the use of historical flight logs, network operation records, and simulated data to train UAV control and wireless network optimization policies, thereby avoiding the risks and costs inherent to real-time trial-and-error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. However, offline RL algorithms are susceptible to value overestimation, particularly for out-of-distribution actions, and often experience unstable training due to their reliance on bootstrapping to propagate returns. Recently, decision transformer (DT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> was introduced to cast the offline RL problem as a sequence modeling task, effectively converting it into a supervised learning problem. By exploiting the powerful sequence representation and long-range dependency modeling capabilities of transformer architectures, DT enables the direct prediction of actions conditioned on desired returns, thereby improving learning stability and mitigating value overestimation. Inspired by the advantages of DT, the work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> made an initial attempt by proposing a DT-based approach to jointly optimize UAV positioning and computation resource allocation in a UAV-assisted edge computing network, effectively improving fairness-based throughput.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the impressive performance of DT, it still faces several challenges when applied to UAV-enabled IoT data collection scenarios. Firstly, DT lacks the stitching ability, and its performance is inherently constrained by the quality of the underlying dataset. When the training data is suboptimal, DT struggles to combine actions from suboptimal trajectories into optimal policies, thereby necessitating expert-level datasets to achieve satisfactory performance. Moreover, The transformer architecture is well known to be data-hungry, requiring massive amounts of training data to achieve satisfactory performance. However, collecting such large-scale and high-quality training datasets is often difficult and costly in UAV-enabled wireless networks. To this end, we propose a critic-regularized DT training paradigm that integrates the sequence modeling strengths of DT with the policy improvement guidance of critic networks, enabling the learning of effective UAV control policies from suboptimal datasets and removing the dependence on expert-level training dataset. In addition, inspired by the impressive few-shot generalization ability of pre-trained large language models (LLMs), we employ a pre-trained LLM as the transformer backbone of the DT and adopt a parameter-efficient fine-tuning approach, namely LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, to rapidly adapt the DT model for UAV control. This design not only reduces data and computational requirements but also facilitates the acquisition of high-quality policies without relying on massive training data.
Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate a UAV trajectory planning and resource allocation problem to maximize the energy efficiency of UAV-assisted data collection. Firstly, the resource allocation subproblem is converted into an equivalent linear programming, which admits an optimal solution in polynomial time. The UAV trajectory planning task is then reformulated as an offline RL problem, and we provide a theoretical analysis of the limitations of existing DT-based methods in solving it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To solve the UAV trajectory planning problem and address the limitations of DT methods, we propose an LLM-empowered critic-regularized DT approach, termed LLM-CRDT. Specifically, we employ two critic networks to estimate the state-action values. Unlike standard DT approaches that rely solely on supervised learning over return-conditioned trajectories, our method introduces a state-action value-based regularization term into the training loss. This encourages the DT model to select actions that align with high-value predictions from the critics, thereby improving policy performance beyond that of the behavior trajectories in the offline dataset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of training LLM-CRDT from scratch, we employ a pre-trained LLM as the transformer backbone of the DT model and fine-tune LLM-CRDT with LoRA, where the pre-trained LLM remains frozen while trainable low-rank matrices are injected into each transformer block. Fine-tuning updates only the input data encoder, LoRA adapters, action decoder, and critic networks, accounting for only a small fraction of the total model parameters. This not only reduces data and computational requirements but also leverages the universal reasoning capabilities of pre-trained LLMs to achieve effective UAV control.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive simulations to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed LLM-CRDT and resource allocation approach. The results show that our method outperforms benchmark online and offline RL approaches. Compared to the current state-of-the-art DT method, it achieves up to a 36.7% improvement in energy efficiency for UAV data collection missions. Moreover, the results demonstrate that our approach is capable of learning effective policies from suboptimal datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as: Section <a href="#S2" title="II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> introduces the system model. In Section <a href="#S3" title="III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, we present the optimal resource allocation algorithm, then reformulates the UAV control problem as an offline RL problem. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> illustrates the proposed LLM-CRDT to learn effective UAV control policies. Simulation results are presented in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Simulation Results ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>. Finally, Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> concludes this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">System Model</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A UAV Movement Model ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this work considers a UAV-enabled IoT network, where a rotary-wing UAV is dispatched to collect data from <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> distributed IoT devices within a rectangular region of side lengths <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby supporting intelligent applications such as environmental monitoring and preventive maintenance. Following prior works, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the system runs in discrete time horizon consisting of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> slots, each with duration <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. The IoT devices are indexed by <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=\{1,2,\cdots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=\{1,2,\cdots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each device <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>) is associated with certain amount of data <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that required to be collected in real-time, and its location information is denoted using three dimensional (3D) Cartesian coordinates, i.e., <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{l}_{n}=(x_{n},y_{n},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒍</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{l}_{n}=(x_{n},y_{n},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are its coordinates along the <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axes, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">UAV Movement Model</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, the UAV is assumed to fly at a constant altitude <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) throughout the entire data collection mission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. It is worth noting that the following proposed approaches can be readily extended to variable-altitude trajectories by introducing an additional control variable for vertical movement.
The UAV’s position in time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{l}_{t}^{\rm{u}}=(x_{t}^{\rm{u}},y_{t}^{\rm{u}},H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒍</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{l}_{t}^{\rm{u}}=(x_{t}^{\rm{u}},y_{t}^{\rm{u}},H)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq x_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq X_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq x_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq X_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq y_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq Y_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq y_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq Y_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is divided into two parts, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}+\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}+\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the UAV’s flight time and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the hovering time for data collection.
At the start of each slot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the UAV determines the flight duration <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>) and flight direction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\theta_{t}\leq 2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\theta_{t}\leq 2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>). It then travels to a new position at an average speed <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq v_{t}\leq V_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq v_{t}\leq V_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>), where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the maximum allowable flight speed determined by the UAV’s hardware limitations.
Thus, the UAV’s horizontal location in slot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.EGx1.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}x_{t}^{\rm{u}}=x_{t-1}^{\rm{u}}+v_{t}\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\cos(\theta_{t}),\\
y_{t}^{\rm{u}}=y_{t-1}^{\rm{u}}+v_{t}\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\sin(\theta_{t}).\end{array}}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}x_{t}^{\rm{u}}=x_{t-1}^{\rm{u}}+v_{t}\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\cos(\theta_{t}),\\
y_{t}^{\rm{u}}=y_{t-1}^{\rm{u}}+v_{t}\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\sin(\theta_{t}).\end{array}}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After reaching the desired position, the UAV hovers there for the remaining time in slot <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to collect data from the IoT devices.
According to the energy consumption model of UAV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, the propulsion power consumption of the UAV with flight speed <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{1}\Big(1+\frac{3v^{2}}{U_{\rm{tip}}^{2}}\Big)+P_{2}\Big(\sqrt{1+\frac{v^{4}}{4v_{0}^{4}}}-\frac{v^{2}}{2v_{0}^{2}}\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>tip</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{1}\Big(1+\frac{3v^{2}}{U_{\rm{tip}}^{2}}\Big)+P_{2}\Big(\sqrt{1+\frac{v^{4}}{4v_{0}^{4}}}-\frac{v^{2}}{2v_{0}^{2}}\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2}d_{0}\rho gAv^{3}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2}d_{0}\rho gAv^{3},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the three terms on the right-hand side correspond to the blade profile power, induced power, and parasite power, respectively.
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the blade profile power during hovering, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\text{tip}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mtext>tip</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\text{tip}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tip speed of the rotor blade. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the induced power and the mean rotor induced velocity in hovering, respectively. For the parasite power, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fuselage drag ratio, air density, rotor solidity, and rotor disc area, respectively.
By setting <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the hovering power consumption of the UAV is given by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}+P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}+P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, the energy consumption of the UAV in time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{t}=\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}P(v_{t})+\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}(P_{1}+P_{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{t}=\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}P(v_{t})+\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}}(P_{1}+P_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13934v1/System_model.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/110;" width="176" height="110" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>The considered UAV-enabled IoT networks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Channel Model</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work adopts the widely used probabilistic line-of-sight (LoS) channel model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> to characterize the large-scale attenuation of the wireless transmission links between the UAV and IoT devices. The probability of establishing a geometrical LoS connection depends on both environment-specific parameters and the UAV’s elevation angle relative to each device.
Specifically, the LoS probability for device <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in slot <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}=\frac{1}{1+a\exp\left(-b(\omega_{n,t}-a)\right)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>LoS</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}=\frac{1}{1+a\exp\left(-b(\omega_{n,t}-a)\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are environment-related parameters, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the elevation angle between the UAV and device <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in time slot <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\omega_{n,t}=\frac{180}{\pi}\arctan\Big(\frac{H}{\sqrt{(x_{t}^{\rm{u}}-x_{n})^{2}+(y_{t}^{\rm{u}}-y_{n})^{2}}}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>180</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>H</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\omega_{n,t}=\frac{180}{\pi}\arctan\Big(\frac{H}{\sqrt{(x_{t}^{\rm{u}}-x_{n})^{2}+(y_{t}^{\rm{u}}-y_{n})^{2}}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, the non-line-of-sight (NLoS) channel probability of device <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in slot <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n,t}^{\rm{NLoS}}=1-P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>NLoS</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>LoS</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n,t}^{\rm{NLoS}}=1-P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The expected channel gain of device <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in slot <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{n,t}=\frac{g_{0}\left(P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}+(1-P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}})\kappa\right)}{\left({H^{2}+(x_{t}^{\rm{u}}-x_{n})^{2}+(y_{t}^{\rm{u}}-y_{n})^{2}}\right)^{\frac{\iota}{2}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>LoS</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>LoS</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>ι</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{n,t}=\frac{g_{0}\left(P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}}+(1-P_{n,t}^{\rm{LoS}})\kappa\right)}{\left({H^{2}+(x_{t}^{\rm{u}}-x_{n})^{2}+(y_{t}^{\rm{u}}-y_{n})^{2}}\right)^{\frac{\iota}{2}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is the path loss exponent, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel gain at the reference distance <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> m, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> captures the additional attenuation incurred under NLoS conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data Collection Model</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an OFDMA-based network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> in which <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> resource blocks (RBs) or chunks support device-to-UAV data transmission, the set of RB indices is <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\cdots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\cdots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In line with prior studies, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, we eliminate inter-device interference by restricting the allocation so that each RB is assigned to at most one device, while each device uses only one RB per slot to communicate with the UAV.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n,m}^{(t)}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n,m}^{(t)}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the RB allocation decision for device <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in time slot <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n,m}^{(t)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n,m}^{(t)}=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that RB <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is allocated to device <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n,m}^{(t)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n,m}^{(t)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. We represent <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t)}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t)}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the RB assignments in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Denoting by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the transmit power of device <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, its achievable transmit rate on RB <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{n,m}^{(t)}=W\log_{2}\Big(1+\frac{p_{n}g_{n,t}}{I_{m,t}+WN_{0}}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{n,m}^{(t)}=W\log_{2}\Big(1+\frac{p_{n}g_{n,t}}{I_{m,t}+WN_{0}}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-RB bandwidth, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise power spectral density, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{m,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{m,t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the co-channel interference from other services sharing the same frequency resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the limited transmit power of IoT devices and substantial path loss, the UAV can only collect data from devices located within its coverage area during the hovering duration of each time slot <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of devices covered by the UAV in time slot <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{N}_{t}^{c}=\left\{n\left|d_{t}^{n}\leq R_{\max},n\in\mathcal{N}\right.\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{N}_{t}^{c}=\left\{n\left|d_{t}^{n}\leq R_{\max},n\in\mathcal{N}\right.\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}^{n}=\sqrt{(x_{n}-x_{t}^{u})^{2}+(y_{n}-y_{t}^{u})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}^{n}=\sqrt{(x_{n}-x_{t}^{u})^{2}+(y_{n}-y_{t}^{u})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the horizontal distance between device <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and the UAV in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximal horizontal coverage range of the UAV decided by the maximal azimuth angle <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{\max}=H\tan\left(\omega_{\max}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{\max}=H\tan\left(\omega_{\max}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Restricted by limited wireless resources, the UAV may not collect data from all devices within its coverage area during each time slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it is crucial to judiciously select the devices for data collection in each slot.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the selection flag for device <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if device <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen by the UAV for data collection, and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Clearly, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\notin\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∉</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\notin\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}_{t}=\{\beta_{n,t}:n\in\mathcal{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}_{t}=\{\beta_{n,t}:n\in\mathcal{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the device selection decision in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each device <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the amount of data collected during slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}=\min\Big\{\beta_{n,t}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},~D_{n,t}\Big\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>collect</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}=\min\Big\{\beta_{n,t}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},~D_{n,t}\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n,t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the remaining data on device <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> at the beginning of slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, it evolves as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{n,t+1}=\max\left\{D_{n,t}-\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}},~0\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>collect</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{n,t+1}=\max\left\{D_{n,t}-\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}},~0\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n,0}=D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n,0}=D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accordingly, the energy efficiency of the UAV in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{t}=\frac{\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}}{E_{t}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>collect</mi></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{t}=\frac{\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}}{E_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">which quantifies data successfully gathered per unit energy in slot <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. A higher value of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates more energy-efficient data collection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work focuses on maximizing the UAV’s energy efficiency during the data collection mission by co-designing the UAV control and resource allocation policies. The optimization problem is formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\left\{\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}},\bm{\beta}_{t},\bm{Z}_{t}\right\}_{t=1}^{T}}~\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}\frac{\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}}{E_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>collect</mi></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\left\{\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}},\bm{\beta}_{t},\bm{Z}_{t}\right\}_{t=1}^{T}}~\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}\frac{\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\Delta_{n,t}^{\rm{collect}}}{E_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S2.Ex2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s. t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq\theta_{t}\leq 2\pi,\forall 1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq\theta_{t}\leq 2\pi,\forall 1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq v_{t}\leq V_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq v_{t}\leq V_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\leq\delta,\forall 1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\leq\delta,\forall 1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq x_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq X_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq x_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq X_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14d)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq y_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq Y_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq y_{t}^{\rm{u}}\leq Y_{\max},\forall 1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14e)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq\beta_{n,t}\leq\mathbbm{1}(n\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}),\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq\beta_{n,t}\leq\mathbbm{1}(n\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}),\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14f)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{n,t}\in\{0,1\},\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{n,t}\in\{0,1\},\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14g)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}z_{n,m}^{(t)}\leq 1,\forall m\in\mathcal{M},1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}z_{n,m}^{(t)}\leq 1,\forall m\in\mathcal{M},1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14h)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}\leq 1,\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}\leq 1,\forall n\in\mathcal{N},1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14i)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{n,m}^{(t)}\in\{0,1\},\forall n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M},1\leq t\leq T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{n,m}^{(t)}\in\{0,1\},\forall n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M},1\leq t\leq T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14j)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">wherer constraints (<a href="#S2.Ex2" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14a</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.Ex4" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14c</span></a>) correspond to the restrictions of UAV control policy, ensuring that the UAV operates within its allowable limits.
(<a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14d</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.Ex6" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14e</span></a>) enforce the UAV’s position to remain within the predefined operational region.
(<a href="#S2.Ex7" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14f</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.Ex8" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14g</span></a>) ensure that only devices within the UAV’s coverage area can be selected for data collection in each slot. Here, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the indicator function, which returns 1 if and only if the condition inside the parentheses is satisfied, and 0 otherwise.
(<a href="#S2.Ex9" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14h</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.Ex10" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14i</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.Ex11" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14j</span></a>) are the resource allocation restrictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Directly solving problem (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) requires complete channel state information between all devices and the UAV across all time slots and for all possible UAV positions. However, this is impractical due to the continuous nature of the UAV’s position space, which contains infinite possible locations. Moreover, accurate channel state information for future time slots cannot be obtained in advance.
To address these challenges, we propose efficient approaches to solve problem (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) in the following sections, without relying on future channel state information of the device-UAV links.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section proposes an effective approach to derive the optimal device selection and resource allocation policy, and reformulate problem (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) as a Markov decision process (MDP) for UAV control. We then highlight the limitations of existing RL and DT algorithms, which motivate the development of the proposed LLM-CRDT to effectively learn UAV control policies in offline settings, as detailed in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to the formulation of problem (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), for any given UAV control policy in arbitrary time slot <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>), the device selection and resource allocation decisions in slot <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> affect only the energy efficiency of this slot and are independent of other slots. Thus, the device selection and resource allocation problem can be decoupled across time slots and solved independently. Accordingly, we focus on deriving the optimal device selection and resource allocation strategy for a specific time slot <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under an arbitrary UAV control policy. Notably, the proposed algorithm can be directly applied to any time slot.
Since <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, once RBs are assigned the device selection is implied.
Accordingly, we decouple the slot-<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> resource allocation subproblem from (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\bm{Z}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\bm{Z}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\min\Big\{\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},~D_{n,t}\Big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\min\Big\{\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t)}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},~D_{n,t}\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s. t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_8},\eqref{cons:P_9},\eqref{cons:P_10}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex9" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14h</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex10" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14i</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex11" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14j</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_8},\eqref{cons:P_9},\eqref{cons:P_10}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Directly solving (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is challenging due to its nonlinear integer structure. We instead cast it as a maximum-weight perfect matching, which can be solved in polynomial time. We therefore convert it to a maximum-weight perfect matching problem, which admits a polynomial-time solution.
Specifically, we define a bipartite graph <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the vertex set is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{N}\cup\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∪</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{N}\cup\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of edges connecting vertices in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the graph <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, each vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to device <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}=\mathcal{M}\cup\mathcal{M}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}=\mathcal{M}\cup\mathcal{M}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is an extended version of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to RB <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> contains virtual vertices introduced to ensure <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|=\left|\mathcal{N}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|=\left|\mathcal{N}\right|</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby forming a balanced bipartite graph. The weight of each edge <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx15.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}\!=\!\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\!\!\!\min\!\big\{r_{n,m}^{(t)}\tau_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\big\},&amp;\text{if}~n\in\!\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}},m\in\!\mathcal{M},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{array}}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}\!=\!\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\!\!\!\min\!\big\{r_{n,m}^{(t)}\tau_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\big\},&amp;\text{if}~n\in\!\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}},m\in\!\mathcal{M},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{array}}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Based on graph <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, we reformulate (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) as a maximum weight perfect bipartite matching problem, where the objective is to seek a perfect matching <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{R}</annotation></semantics></math> maximizing <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{(n,m)\in\bm{R}}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{(n,m)\in\bm{R}}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We introduce the binary connector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{n,m}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{n,m}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> for edge <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{n,m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{n,m}=1</annotation></semantics></math> represents assigning RB <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to device <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{n,m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{n,m}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Hence, the bipartite matching problem is formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\{c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\{c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}}\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s. t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}c_{n,m}=1,\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}c_{n,m}=1,\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}=1,\forall n\in\mathcal{N}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}=1,\forall n\in\mathcal{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{n,m}\in\left\{0,1\right\},\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}},n\in\mathcal{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{n,m}\in\left\{0,1\right\},\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}},n\in\mathcal{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">However, finding the optimal solution to problem (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) remains non-trivial, as it is an integer programming problem. By relaxing the integer constraint (<a href="#S3.Ex15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18c</span></a>), problem (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) can be transformed into the following linear programming:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\{c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}}\}_{n=1}^{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\{c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}}\}_{n=1}^{N}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{\left|\bar{\mathcal{M}}\right|}c_{n,m}\Omega_{n,m}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s. t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_dm1},\eqref{cons:P_dm2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.Ex13" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18a</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.Ex14" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18b</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_dm1},\eqref{cons:P_dm2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq c_{n,m}\leq 1,\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}},n\in\mathcal{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq c_{n,m}\leq 1,\forall m\in\bar{\mathcal{M}},n\in\mathcal{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmrem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), each row associated with (<a href="#S3.Ex13" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18a</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.Ex14" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18b</span></a>) has exactly one coefficient equal to <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, implying total unimodularity of the coefficient matrix (since every square submatrix has determinant <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>).
According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) attains an integral optimum that is also optimal for the original problem (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Remark <a href="#Thmrem1" title="Remark 1. ‣ III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees that solving (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) directly recovers the optimum of (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>). This work employs the current matrix multiplication time algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> to find the optimal solution of problem (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), which has a time complexity of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}((N^{2+1/6})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}((N^{2+1/6})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> as problem (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) involves <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> variables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above analysis, problem (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) can be efficiently solved to obtain the optimal device selection and resource allocation decisions. For clarity, we present the detailed solution procedure in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Firstly, we transform problem (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) into its equivalent bipartite graph matching form, i.e., (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), which involves calculating the edge weights and verifying the feasibility of assigning each RB to each device. This incurs a time complexity of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NM)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, we convert (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) into its equivalent problem, i.e., (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), and find the optimal resource allocation decision <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimal device selection policy is then computed as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{n,t}^{*}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{n,t}^{*}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the overall time complexity of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NM+(N^{2+1/6})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NM+(N^{2+1/6})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Optimal Device Selection and RB Allocation</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Formulate the RB allocation problem (</span><a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a><span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Construct a bipartite graph </span><math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑮</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and compute the weights of each edge in </span><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> based on (</span><a href="#S3.EGx15" title="III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a><span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">).
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Construct the linear programming problem of the bipartite graph matching problem, i.e., (</span><a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a><span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Solve problem (</span><a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a><span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">) and obtain the optimal bipartite perfect matching </span><math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{c}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒄</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{c}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Calculate the optimal RB allocation decision as </span><math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t),*}=c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t),*}=c_{n,m}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Calculate the optimal device selection decision as </span><math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}_{t}^{*}=\{\alpha_{n,t}^{*}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}:\forall n\in\mathcal{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜶</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}_{t}^{*}=\{\alpha_{n,t}^{*}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}:\forall n\in\mathcal{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Return the optimal device selection decision </span><math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜷</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and the optimal RB allocation decision </span><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Transformation and Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above analysis, the optimal device selection <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}=\{\beta_{n,t}^{*}:n\in\mathcal{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}=\{\beta_{n,t}^{*}:n\in\mathcal{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and optimal resource allocation policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t),*}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}=\{z_{n,m}^{(t),*}:n\in\mathcal{N},m\in\mathcal{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> under any given UAV control policy can now be obtained using Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. By substituting <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> into the original problem (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), we reduce it to the following UAV control problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\max_{\left\{\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\right\}_{t=1}^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\max_{\left\{\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}\right\}_{t=1}^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}\!\frac{\sum\limits_{n=1}^{N}\!\min\!\Big\{\!\sum\limits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\Big\}}{E_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}\!\frac{\sum\limits_{n=1}^{N}\!\min\!\Big\{\!\sum\limits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\Big\}}{E_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s. t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_1},\eqref{cons:P_2},\eqref{cons:P_3},\eqref{cons:P_4},\eqref{cons:P_5}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex2" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14a</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex3" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14b</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex4" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14c</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14d</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Ex6" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14e</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eqref{cons:P_1},\eqref{cons:P_2},\eqref{cons:P_3},\eqref{cons:P_4},\eqref{cons:P_5}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Problem (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) is intrinsically a sequential decision-making problem, which is impractical to solve directly as it requires precise channel state information for all device-UAV links over the entire time horizon and across all possible UAV positions. Moreover, even with full channel state information, the long-term and non-convex nature of (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) would still lead to significant computational challenges.
To tackle these issues, we reformulate problem (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) as a MDP and employ RL algorithms to learn a satisfactory UAV control policy.
Specifically, we define the MDP as a tuple <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R},\gamma\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R},\gamma\right)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state space, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action space, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition model, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor. In each time slot <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, one can choose an action <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the current state <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. This action is then applied to the environment, resulting in a transition to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t+1}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t+1}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and returning <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=\mathcal{R}(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=\mathcal{R}(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, the state, action, and reward function are designed as follows:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">State</em>: In each time slot <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the state <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the horizontal position of the UAV, the remaining data amount of devices, and the coverage indicator of devices, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{s}_{t}\!=\!(x_{t}^{\rm{u}},y_{t}^{\rm{u}},\!D_{1,t},\cdots,D_{N,t},\!\mathbbm{1}(1\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}),\!\cdots\!,\!\mathbbm{1}(N\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{s}_{t}\!=\!(x_{t}^{\rm{u}},y_{t}^{\rm{u}},\!D_{1,t},\cdots,D_{N,t},\!\mathbbm{1}(1\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}}),\!\cdots\!,\!\mathbbm{1}(N\in\mathcal{N}_{t}^{\rm{c}})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Action</em>: The UAV control action in each time slot <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, comprises the flight direction, speed, and flight time, defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{a}_{t}=(\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>fly</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{a}_{t}=(\theta_{t},v_{t},\delta_{t}^{\rm{fly}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy the constraints (<a href="#S2.Ex2" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14a</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.Ex6" title="In II-D Problem Formulation ‣ II System Model ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14e</span></a>) in problem (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reward</em>: According to the objective of problem (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), we set the per-slot reward to the energy efficiency, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!r_{t}=\frac{\sum\limits_{n=1}^{N}\min\Big\{\sum\limits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\Big\}}{E_{t}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>hover</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!r_{t}=\frac{\sum\limits_{n=1}^{N}\min\Big\{\sum\limits_{m=1}^{M}z_{n,m}^{(t),*}r_{n,m}^{(t)}\delta_{t}^{\rm{hover}},D_{n,t}\Big\}}{E_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above defined MDP, various online RL approaches, such as SAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and TD3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, can be utilized to learn effective UAV control policies.
These algorithms continuously interact with the environment and update their policy to maximize the long-term cumulative reward, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, their interactive nature limits practical deployment, particularly for UAV control tasks where real-time exploration is often infeasible or costly. In addition, online RL requires persistent access to the environment for hyperparameter tuning and policy refinement, which is both resource- and time-intensive.
Although offline RL methods, such as temporal-difference (TD) learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, can learn effective policies entirely from pre-collected dataset without interacting with the environment, they often suffer from unstable training due to their reliance on bootstrapping to propagate returns. Furthermore, the requirement to discount future rewards can induce undesirable short-sighted behaviors, limiting the agent’s ability to plan over long horizons.
To overcome these issues, the DT framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> treats the offline RL problem as a generic conditional sequence modeling task, effectively converting it into a supervised learning problem. This enables DT to handle long sequences and avoid stability issues associated with boostrapping.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13934v1/decision_transformer.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/106;" width="215" height="106" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The architecture of decision transformer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the offline setting, interactions with the environment are no longer available. Agents can only learn from a pre-collected trajectory dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories generated by an unknown behavior policy <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\rm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\rm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\{1,2,\cdots,E\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\{1,2,\cdots,E\}</annotation></semantics></math>) is an ordered sequence of return-to-go (RTG) values, states, and actions over the time horizon, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e}=(\widehat{R}_{1},\bm{s}_{1},\bm{a}_{1},\widehat{R}_{2},\bm{s}_{2},\bm{a}_{2},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{T},\bm{s}_{T},\bm{a}_{T})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e}=(\widehat{R}_{1},\bm{s}_{1},\bm{a}_{1},\widehat{R}_{2},\bm{s}_{2},\bm{a}_{2},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{T},\bm{s}_{T},\bm{a}_{T}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the RTG in time slot <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the sum of future rewards from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to the end of the episode, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{R}_{t}=\sum\nolimits_{t^{\prime}=t}^{T}r_{t^{\prime}},\forall t\in\{1,2,\cdots,T\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{R}_{t}=\sum\nolimits_{t^{\prime}=t}^{T}r_{t^{\prime}},\forall t\in\{1,2,\cdots,T\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given the offline trajectory dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can train a DT model to learn the UAV control policy. The architecture of the DT model is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which consists of three components: a data encoder module, a causal transformer module composed of a stack of transformer layers, and an action decoder module.
For each trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and each time slot <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we feed the most recent <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps of trajectory data into the DT model, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e,t}=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1},\bm{s}_{t-K+1},\bm{a}_{t-K+1},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e,t}=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1},\bm{s}_{t-K+1},\bm{a}_{t-K+1},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is referred to as the context length. The input sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is first processed by the data encoder module, which comprises three linear layers and a learnable time embedding layer.
The time embedding layer encodes the time information of each step <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-K+1\leq i\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-K+1\leq i\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>) into a vector whose dimension matches the hidden dimension of the transformer module, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\rm{trans}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\rm{trans}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden dimension of the transformer module.
Each of the three linear layers maps the RTG <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the state <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the action <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to vector representations, also matching the hidden dimension of the transformer.
The time embedding is then added into each representation to form the input tokens:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx25.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\widehat{R}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\widehat{R}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}},\\
\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{s}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}},\\
\bm{a}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{a}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}}.\end{array}}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>trans</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\widehat{R}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\widehat{R}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}},\\
\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{s}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}},\\
\bm{a}_{i}^{\rm{v}}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{a}_{i})+\bm{t}_{i}^{\rm{v}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\rm{trans}}}.\end{array}}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After encoding <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a token sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{v}}=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\bm{s}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\bm{a}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t}^{\rm{v}},\bm{s}_{t}^{\rm{v}},\bm{a}_{t}^{\rm{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{v}}=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\bm{s}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\bm{a}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{v}},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t}^{\rm{v}},\bm{s}_{t}^{\rm{v}},\bm{a}_{t}^{\rm{v}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then fed into the transformer module and output a corresponding sequence of predicted tokens, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{out}}={\rm{Transformer}}(\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Transformer</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{out}}={\rm{Transformer}}(\bm{\tau}_{e,t}^{\rm{v}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\bm{s}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\bm{a}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t}^{\rm{out}},\bm{s}_{t}^{\rm{out}},\bm{a}_{t}^{\rm{out}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\widehat{R}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\bm{s}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\bm{a}_{t-K+1}^{\rm{out}},\cdots,\widehat{R}_{t}^{\rm{out}},\bm{s}_{t}^{\rm{out}},\bm{a}_{t}^{\rm{out}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, each output token <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>out</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-K+1\leq i\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-K+1\leq i\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>) is passed to the action decoder, which consists of a linear layer, to predict the action for timestep <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{out}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>out</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}={\rm{Linear}}(\bm{s}_{i}^{\rm{out}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">In summary, given a trajectory segment <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the DT model outputs a sequence of predicted actions <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\bm{a}}_{t-K+1},\ldots,\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\bm{a}}_{t-K+1},\ldots,\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted action at time step <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
The learning objective of DT model <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, is to predict next action <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from trajectory prefix and current state <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the true action <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target, written as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})=\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}\left\|\bm{a}_{i}-\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}\right\|^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>DT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})=\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}\left\|\bm{a}_{i}-\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">After training, the DT model can be deployed to interact with the environment and generate UAV control actions in an autoregressive manner.
For DT framework, we have the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the above-defined MDP is <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-deterministic, i.e., <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr(r_{t}\neq\mathcal{R}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})~{\rm{or}}~\bm{s}_{t+1}\neq\mathcal{P}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\left|\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\right.)\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>or</mi><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(r_{t}\neq\mathcal{R}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})~{\rm{or}}~\bm{s}_{t+1}\neq\mathcal{P}(\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\left|\bm{s}_{t},\bm{a}_{t})\right.)\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> at all <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the reward function <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and environment dynamics <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\bm{\tau})=\sum\nolimits_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\bm{\tau})=\sum\nolimits_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a dataset <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> pre-collected by behavior policy <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\rm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\rm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains a sufficient number of trajectories whose returns match the DT’s conditioning values <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e, <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr(g(\bm{\tau})=\hat{R}_{1}\left|\bm{s}_{1},\bm{\tau}\in\mathcal{D}\right.)\geq\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝛕</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(g(\bm{\tau})=\hat{R}_{1}\left|\bm{s}_{1},\bm{\tau}\in\mathcal{D}\right.)\geq\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> for all initial states <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the DT policy <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> learned from this dataset satisfy:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\tau}\sim\pi_{\rm{B}}}\left[g(\bm{\tau})\right]-\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\tau}\sim\pi_{\bm{\theta}}}\left[g(\bm{\tau})\right]\leq\varepsilon\Big(\frac{1}{\zeta}+2\Big)T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ζ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\tau}\sim\pi_{\rm{B}}}\left[g(\bm{\tau})\right]-\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\tau}\sim\pi_{\bm{\theta}}}\left[g(\bm{\tau})\right]\leq\varepsilon\Big(\frac{1}{\zeta}+2\Big)T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A0.SS1" title="-A Proof of Theorem ‣ VI Conclusion ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-A</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">According to Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, training a DT model with the loss function <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\rm{DT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>DT</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\rm{DT}}</annotation></semantics></math> drives the learned policy to gradually converge toward the behavior policy <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\rm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\rm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this convergence inherently restricts the learned DT policy from surpassing the performance of the underlying behavior policy <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\rm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\rm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> that used to generate the trajectories in the offline dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, training exclusively with the <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\rm{DT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>DT</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\rm{DT}}</annotation></semantics></math> limits the DT’s ability to stitch together high-reward actions across different trajectories, leaving the learned DT policy <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> largely biased toward actions observed during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limitations of DT approaches discussed above, this section presents an LLM-empowered critic-regularized DT framework, i.e., LLM-CRDT, to solve problem (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) and facilitate efficient online UAV control. Specifically, we first propose a critic-regularized DT learning paradigm that endows the DT model with stitching capabilities. Then, we employ a pre-trained LLM as the transformer backbone of the DT model and employ LoRA for fine-tuning, thereby exploiting the LLM’s few-shot generalization ability to further enhance UAV control performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.13934v1/LLM_CRDT_finetune.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/176;" width="234" height="176" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.13934v1/LLM_CRDT_inference.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/89;" width="229" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>The architecture of the proposed LLM-CRDT: (a) Fine-tuning on an offline trajectory dataset, (b) Inference to generate action and interact with environment.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the stitching capability inherent in actor-critic methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we propose a critic-regularized DT learning paradigm, which integrates the sequence modeling capacities of DT with the policy improvement guidance provided by critic networks, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Unlike the standard DT approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> that rely solely on supervised learning over return-conditioned trajectories, our approach introduces a state-action value-based regularization term into the training loss. This encourages the model to select actions that align with high value predictions from the critic, thereby improving policy performance beyond that of the behavior trajectories in the offline dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we draw inspiration from the actor-critic algorithms (such as SAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and TD3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) and employ two critic networks, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with their corresponding target networks, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to learn accurate estimates of the state-action value function.
In addition, we employ a DT model <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, to predict actions.
Following the common practice in conventional RL approaches, we maintain a target DT model <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance the stability of the learning process.
Considering that the DT policy conditions on past trajectory information, we estimate the state-action values via the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-step Bellman backup, which has been shown to outperform the 1-step approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Given a trajectory segment <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In III-B Problem Transformation and Analysis ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>), we use the target DT policy <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the action at time step <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then compute the TD target for the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th step (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-K+1\leq i\leq t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-K+1\leq i\leq t-1</annotation></semantics></math>) as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{Q}_{i}=\sum\limits_{j=i}^{t-1}\gamma^{j-i}r_{j}+\gamma^{t-i}\mathop{\min}\limits_{h\in\{1,2\}}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime}}(\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">min</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{Q}_{i}=\sum\limits_{j=i}^{t-1}\gamma^{j-i}r_{j}+\gamma^{t-i}\mathop{\min}\limits_{h\in\{1,2\}}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime}}(\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Next, we use the critic networks <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the state-action values at each time step <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and their parameters are optimized by minimizing the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L(\bm{\phi}_{h})=\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t-1}{\Big\|\hat{Q}_{i}-Q_{\bm{\phi}_{h}}(\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})\Big\|^{2}},h\in\{1,2\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L(\bm{\phi}_{h})=\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t-1}{\Big\|\hat{Q}_{i}-Q_{\bm{\phi}_{h}}(\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})\Big\|^{2}},h\in\{1,2\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During the DT model training, we incorporate a state-action value loss term to regularize the DT towards sampling high-value actions. Specifically, we first use the DT model <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the actions in each time step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{t-K+1,\cdots,t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{t-K+1,\cdots,t\}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t-K+1},\cdots,\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t-K+1},\cdots,\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then, we use the critic network <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate the state-action values for these predicted actions, i.e, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}):\forall i\in\{t-K+1,\cdots,t\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}):\forall i\in\{t-K+1,\cdots,t\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the DT model is optimized by minimizing the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})+\lambda\cdot L_{Q}(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>DT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})+\lambda\cdot L_{Q}(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})-\lambda\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>DT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=L_{\rm{DT}}(\bm{\theta})-\lambda\sum\limits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{Q}(\bm{\theta})=-\sum\nolimits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{Q}(\bm{\theta})=-\sum\nolimits_{i=t-K+1}^{t}Q_{\bm{\phi}_{1}}(\bm{s}_{i},\hat{\bm{a}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization term to evaluate the DT policy and promotes high-reward actions, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter used to balance the trajectory modeling loss and the regularization loss.
By training with the loss function (<a href="#S4.Ex20" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>), the DT model acquires stitching capabilities and is able to outperform the underlying behavior policy used to collect the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, to stabilize learning process, we adopt the soft-update strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> to update the target critic networks (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the target policy network <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. With mixing coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the target networks are updated as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.EGx33.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.EGx33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\bm{\theta}^{\prime}=\rho\bm{\theta}+(1-\rho)\bm{\theta}^{\prime},\\
\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime}=\rho\bm{\phi}_{h}+(1-\rho)\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime},h\in\{1,2\}.\end{array}}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{{\begin{array}[]{*{20}{c}}\bm{\theta}^{\prime}=\rho\bm{\theta}+(1-\rho)\bm{\theta}^{\prime},\\
\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime}=\rho\bm{\phi}_{h}+(1-\rho)\bm{\phi}_{h}^{\prime},h\in\{1,2\}.\end{array}}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer</span></h3>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Fine-tuning of LLM-CRDT</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Input</span><span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: Offline trajectory dataset </span><math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, context length </span><math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, soft update coefficient </span><math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, LoRA rank </span><math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, scaling factor </span><math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, batch size </span><math id="alg2.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Create LoRA adapters for the query, key, value projection layers and attention output layer in each transformer block of the LLM.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Freeze the LLM backbone, enable the parameters of data encoder, LoRA adapters, action decoder, and critics to be trainable.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l5.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Randomly sample a batch of </span><math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l5.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> trajectories </span><math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l5.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> from </span><math id="alg2.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l5.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l6.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">For each trajectory </span><math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, randomly select a time slot </span><math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and extract the corresponding </span><math id="alg2.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">-step segment </span><math id="alg2.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, forming a batch of trajectory segments, i.e., </span><math id="alg2.l6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}\}_{e=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}\}_{e=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l6.3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l7.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l7.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pass </span><math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l7.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> through the target DT </span><math id="alg2.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l7.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> to predict actions for time slot </span><math id="alg2.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l7.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, i.e., </span><math id="alg2.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,t_{e}}:e\in\{1,\cdots,B\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,t_{e}}:e\in\{1,\cdots,B\}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l7.3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l8.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l8.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Compute the TD target for every time slot of each trajectory segment </span><math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l8.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> based on (</span><a href="#S4.E34" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a><span id="alg2.l8.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">).</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l9.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l9.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l9.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Compute the critic losses for </span><math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l9.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="alg2.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l9.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> based on (</span><a href="#S4.E35" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a><span id="alg2.l9.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">), and perform gradient descent to optimize them.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l10.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l10.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l10.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Input the </span><math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l10.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> into the DT model </span><math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l10.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> to compute the predicted actions, i.e., </span><math id="alg2.l10.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}=\{\hat{\bm{a}}_{h,r}:1\leq h\leq B,t_{h}-K+1\leq r\leq t_{h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}=\{\hat{\bm{a}}_{h,r}:1\leq h\leq B,t_{h}-K+1\leq r\leq t_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l10.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l11.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l11.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l11.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l11.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Compute the DT loss based on (</span><a href="#S4.Ex20" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a><span id="alg2.l11.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">), and optimize it via gradient descent.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l12.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg2.l12.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg2.l12.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg2.l12.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">update the target critic networks and target actor network based on (</span><a href="#S4.EGx33" title="IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a><span id="alg2.l12.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">).</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg2.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">until</span><span id="alg2.l13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> The model is converged.
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Up to now, the proposed critic-regularized DT gains stitching capability. However, transformers are known to be data-hungry and usually require massive amounts of training data to achieve satisfactory performance. As a result, training the DT model from scratch can be both challenging and computationally expensive. To this end, this subsection proposes LLM-CRDT to leverage the few-shot generalization capabilities of pre-trained LLMs to enhance the DT model for UAV control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the standard DT framework in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, we adopt GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> as the transformer module in our proposed LLM-CRDT. However, unlike prior DT works (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>) that randomly initialize model parameters and train DT models from scratch, we initialize the transformer with pre-trained GPT-2 parameters. This enables the DT model to exploit the general knowledge encoded in LLMs, thereby enhancing action prediction. Note that the transformer module in DT can be flexibly replaced with other casual LLMs, such as LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, with the choice of LLM depending on the tradeoff between computational cost and performance requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After initialization, we fine-tune LLM-CRDT, which consists of the data encoder, the pre-trained LLM, the action decoder, and the critic networks. Notably, the pre-trained LLM accounts for the majority of the model parameters, whereas the parameter sizes of the other modules are comparatively negligible. Given the large scale of LLMs, full-parameter fine-tuning is computationally expensive and risks overwriting the valuable knowledge embedded in the pre-trained LLM, potentially diminishing its few-shot generalization capabilities. To this end, we adopt a parameter-efficient fine-tuning approach, i.e., LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, to efficiently adapt the pre-trained LLM for UAV control, thereby substantially reducing the number of trainable parameters. Specifically, we apply LoRA to the query, key, and value projection layers, as well as the self-attention output layer in each transformer block of the LLM. For a given layer with parameter matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\rm{LLM}}\times d_{\rm{LLM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\rm{LLM}}\times d_{\rm{LLM}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\rm{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\rm{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the LLM’s hidden dimension. LoRA injects trainable low-rank matrices <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\rm{LLM}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\rm{LLM}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\rm{LLM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\rm{LLM}}}</annotation></semantics></math> into the layer. The original weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is then modified as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{W}\leftarrow\mathbf{W}+\frac{\alpha}{r}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁𝐀</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{W}\leftarrow\mathbf{W}+\frac{\alpha}{r}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the LoRA adapter rank and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls its influence. During fine-tuning, the weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> remains frozen, while only the LoRA adapter matrices <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated. Typically, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized with Gaussian distribution values, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized to zeros. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll d_{\rm{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll d_{\rm{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of trainable parameters introduced by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2rd_{\rm{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2rd_{\rm{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is significantly smaller than that of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\rm{LLM}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>LLM</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\rm{LLM}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Consequently, LoRA enables efficient and cost-effective fine-tuning. Furthermore, by keeping the original LLM weights fixed, LoRA mitigates catastrophic forgetting, thereby enabling us to retain and exploit the universal reasoning and generalization capabilities of the pre-trained LLM for UAV control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With the assistance of LoRA, we fine-tune the LLM-CRDT to learn the UAV control policy from an offline trajectory dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories. In particular, the LLM weights are frozen, while only the parameters of the data encoder, LoRA adapters, action decoder, and critic networks are updated, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In each fine-tuning epoch, we randomly sample a batch of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e}\}_{e=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each trajectory <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, we randomly select a time slot <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and extract the corresponding <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-step segment <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> ending at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby forming a batch of trajectory segments <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}\}_{e=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}=\{\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}\}_{e=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then pass <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> through the target DT model to predict the action at time step <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> for each trajectory segment <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{e,t_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,t_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,t_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following that, we compute TD targets for each time step based on (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), evaluate the critic losses via (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), and optimize the critic networks via gradient descent. Subsequently, we feed <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> into the DT model to obtain the predicted actions for each time step of each trajectory segment, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{e,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=1,\dots,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=1,\dots,B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=t_{e}-K+1,\dots,t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=t_{e}-K+1,\dots,t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we compute the loss of the DT model based on (<a href="#S4.Ex20" title="In IV-A Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) and perform gradient descent to update the parameters of DT model. For clarity, the detailed steps for fine-tuning the proposed LLM-CRDT model are summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-B LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inference of LLM empowered Decision Transformer</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After fine-tuning, we deploy the proposed LLM-CRDT model for online inference to control the UAV and complete the data collection mission. In the deployment phase, only the trained DT model <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is required, while the target DT model and critic networks are utilized solely during fine-tuning and are not involved in inference. During deployment, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the environment in an autoregressive loop using a context window of length <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the context length adopted in fine-tuning phase, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Inference of LLM-CRDT for UAV control</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Input</span><span id="alg3.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">: Trained DT model </span><math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, target RTG </span><math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, context length </span><math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Initialize the environment and obtain the initial state </span><math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Initialize the trajectory history as </span><math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}=\{(\bm{s}_{1},\hat{R}_{1})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}=\{(\bm{s}_{1},\hat{R}_{1})\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and time slot </span><math id="alg3.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="alg3.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1:T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1:T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg3.l4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg3.l5.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg3.l5.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Construct the input sequence </span><math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> by concatenating the </span><math id="alg3.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> most-recent completed triples </span><math id="alg3.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{R}_{i},\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{R}_{i},\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg3.l5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=t-K,\cdots,t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=t-K,\cdots,t-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> in </span><math id="alg3.l5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, followed by the current pair </span><math id="alg3.l5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l5.3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Left-pad the input sequence </span><math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> if </span><math id="alg3.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Input </span><math id="alg3.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> into the DT model and obtain predicted action </span><math id="alg3.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
<span id="alg3.l8.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:331.1pt;">
<span id="alg3.l8.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="alg3.l8.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Execute action </span><math id="alg3.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l8.3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> in the environment, apply Algorithm </span><a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-A Optimal Device Selection and Resource Allocation ‣ III Optimal Resource Allocation and Problem Transformation ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="alg3.l8.3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for device selection and resource allocation, and then obtain the next state </span><math id="alg3.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l8.3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and reward </span><math id="alg3.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l8.3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l9.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Update the RTG as </span><math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{t+1}=\hat{R}_{t}-r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t+1}=\hat{R}_{t}-r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l10.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Append the triplets </span><math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> to trajectory </span><math id="alg3.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l11.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg3.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">if</span><span id="alg3.l11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> termination condition is met </span><span id="alg3.l11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">then</span><span id="alg3.l11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg3.l12.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Break the circulation.
  </span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of an episode, we initialize the environment to obtain the initial state <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and specify a desired RTG <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is usually set as the maximum possible return in the training dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Then, we perform the DT model <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate UAV control actions in each slot to complete the data collection task.
Specifically, in each time slot <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, given the current state <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and RTG <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{R}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{R}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assemble the model’s input sequence as the most recent <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> triplets <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{R}_{i},\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{R}_{i},\bm{s}_{i},\bm{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> up to time slot <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by the pair <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
If fewer than <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> time slots are available, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;K</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence is left-padded and masked.
The input sequence is then processed in the same manner as during fine-tuning: it is first encoded by the data encoder, passed through the LLM, and finally decoded by the action decoder to yield the predicted action <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{a}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The action is executed in the environment, producing the next state <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and reward <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RTG is subsequently updated as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{R}_{t+1}=\hat{R}_{t}-r_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{R}_{t+1}=\hat{R}_{t}-r_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the new triplet <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{R}_{t},\bm{s}_{t},\hat{\bm{a}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is appended to the trajectory history and the context window is advanced to retain at most <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> steps.
This process is repeated until the episode terminates.
For clarity, we summarize the detailed steps of the LLM-CRDT inference for UAV control in Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ IV-C Inference of LLM empowered Decision Transformer ‣ IV LLM-Empowered Critic-Regularized Decision Transformer for UAV Control ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Simulation Results</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section evaluates the effectiveness of the proposed LLM-CRDT and resource allocation approach. In the simulations, we consider the devices are randomly distributed within a rectangular area with side length <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\max}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\max}=1</annotation></semantics></math> km and <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\max}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\max}=1</annotation></semantics></math> km. The initial data volume of each device <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is randomly selected from <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5D_{\max},D_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5D_{\max},D_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Unless specified otherwise, the default settings of the UAV-enabled IoT network are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V Simulation Results ‣ Large Language Model-Empowered Decision Transformer for UAV-Enabled Data Collection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, which follow widely adopted configurations for UAV-enabled IoT networks in prior works, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In the proposed LLM-CRDT model, GPT-2 serves as the LLM backbone, while both critic networks adopt a three-layer MLP architecture with Mish activations and 512 hidden units per layer. For dataset preparation, a behavior policy is first trained using the SAC algorithm, after which an offline dataset of 1000 trajectories is collected by rolling out this policy in the environment. The fine-tuning of LLM-CRDT is performed over 1000 epochs with a context length of <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=20</annotation></semantics></math>, a LoRA rank of <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math>, a LoRA scaling factor of <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=32</annotation></semantics></math>, a batch size of <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=128</annotation></semantics></math>, a soft update coefficient of <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.005</annotation></semantics></math>, and a loss coefficient of <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the DT model and the critic networks are optimized using the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
